\par }}\dpx4536\dpy336\dpxsize1152\dpysize576\dpfillfgcr255\dpfillfgcg255\dpfillfgcb255\dpfillbgcr255\dpfillbgcg255\dpfillbgcb255\dpfillpat1\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par Over the years we have seen many administrators, who were stuck using Microsoft and Netscape servers with export-crippled security, put a copy of a full-strength SSL Apache-based server onto a spare machine to act as an HTTPS-to-HTTP gateway.
\par Now because simple static pages and images are very quick to serve, the SSL gateway can be configured to serve these pages itself and just pass on other requests.  With all cryptography now handled by a single machi
ne, one or more cryptographic accelerator boards could be attached to it to cope with the necessary load. Using this mirror proxy approach works well for balancing SSL requests; you end up with a single machine that can take in SSL requests, establish the
 sessions as required, and let back-end machines actually perform the other web operations.  It is this theme of \ldblquote componentisation\rdblquote 
 that we are converging towards, where the resources required to service the CGI or ASP requests can be assessed independently of the resources required to deal with the expected SSL overheads.
\par However this approach presents a risk-management issue and also doesn\rquote t scale well enough for very large sites:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s16 \fs20\lang2057\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 1.\tab}}\pard \s16\fi-180\li810\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx450{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls10\adjustright {
There will be a limit to the number of cryptographic accelerators you can add to a single machine.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s16 \fs20\lang2057\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 2.\tab}}\pard \s16\fi-180\li810\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx450{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls10\adjustright {
The internal processing and system-level considerations of that one machine will eventually become a bottleneck.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s16 \fs20\lang2057\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 3.\tab}}\pard \s16\fi-180\li810\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx450{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls10\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls10\adjustright {
The architecture becomes critically dependent on this one gateway - there is no redundancy.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\li426\sb240\sl-280\slmult0\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\kerning28\cgrid {Hardware Gateway
\par }\pard\plain \s16\li432\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {At least one manufacturer has taken the idea of a software gateway one step further by producing a stand-alone hardware solution.  The Intel \lquote iPivot\rquote 
 is a self-contained unit that can be configured in a similar way to the software gateway and includes some crypto acceleration.  It normally doesn\rquote 
t have the ability to serve pages locally, and is less easy to upgrade than a standard Apache proxying system. Using a software gateway solution also has a scalability advantage in that you can just switch to a faster processo
r or add more accelerators as and when required whereas hardware gateways are a little less upgradable when resourcing requirements can change rapidly.  Software gateways are also more customisable; allowing client certificate rules to be set 
up and maintained in-sync with the web-server configurations, and are able to pass on headers to the back-end servers with details such as the encryption algorithms used or details of client certificates.  
\par }\pard\plain \s1\qj\sb360\sa120\sl-360\slmult0\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw15\brsp60 \outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\caps\f1\fs28\kerning28\cgrid {Solving the problem
\par }\pard\plain \s16\li432\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {To summarise, having examined the current solutions to SSL-enabling Apache, we have identified the following as major limitations:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s32 \fs20\lang2057\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 a)\tab}}\pard\plain \s32\fi-270\li810\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx810{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls12\adjustright 
\fs20\lang2057\cgrid {Session caching needs to be more flexible and not localised to each web server.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s32 \fs20\lang2057\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 b)\tab}}\pard \s32\fi-270\li810\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx810{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls12\adjustright {
Resourcing of key operations (such as RSA and DSA) needs to be more scalable and independent of the web server. It should be possible to provide operating resources for each separately.  The 
systems best suited to performing key operations are often not the preferred platform for running web servers and web-applications.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s32 \fs20\lang2057\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 c)\tab}}\pard \s32\fi-270\li810\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx810{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls12\adjustright {
Private keys and key operations need to be protected from web server applications.  Also, it is important to distinguish between access rights to administrate the web server and access to the private keys.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s32 \fs20\lang2057\cgrid \hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 d)\tab}}\pard \s32\fi-270\li810\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx810{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls12\pnrnot0\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta )}}\ls12\adjustright {
The current architectures don't allow the resourcing of systems to follow the demographics of a site\rquote 
s traffic; certain platforms may be better suited to different tasks, e.g. you may wish to perform RSA operations on Intel platforms but run your web servers on Sparc platforms. Similarly, we may wish 
to increase our capacity for performing key operations even if our web serving and session caching are sufficiently resourced.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\qj\li426\sb240\sl-280\slmult0\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\kerning28\cgrid {Our solution
\par }\pard\plain \s16\li432\sa120\nowidctlpar\widctlpar{\*\pn \pnlvlcont\ilvl0\ls0\pnrnot0\pndec }\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\lang1024 
